---
import standards from '../../data/standards.json';
const SITE = 'https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com';
const rows = standards.standards;
---
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Battery Testing Standards Index — World Battery Hub</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Index of battery testing standards: SAE, IEC, JIS, DIN, EN, UL, IEEE, UN and IATA — scope, region, chemistry and the object each standard tests." />
    <link rel="canonical" href={`${SITE}/standards/`} />
    <style>
      body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 0; color: #1e293b; line-height: 1.6; }
      .container { max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 20px; }
      h1, h2 { color: #0f1f3d; }
      a { color: #1d4ed8; }
      .muted { color: #64748b; font-size: 0.85rem; }
      table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.95rem; }
      th, td { text-align: left; padding: 8px 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid #e5e9f0; vertical-align: top; }
      th { color: #0f1f3d; }
    </style>
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "CollectionPage",
      "@id": `${SITE}/standards/#page`,
      "url": `${SITE}/standards/`,
      "name": "Battery Testing Standards Index",
      "isPartOf": { "@id": `${SITE}/` },
      "mainEntity": {
        "@type": "ItemList",
        "numberOfItems": rows.length,
        "itemListElement": rows.map((s, i) => ({
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": i + 1,
          "url": `${SITE}/standards/${s.id}/`,
          "name": `${s.code} — ${s.object}`
        }))
      }
    })} />
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="container">
      <p class="muted"><a href="/">Battery Testing</a> › Standards index</p>
      <h1>Battery testing standards index</h1>
      <p>Every standard in the reference database — region, chemistry and the performance or safety object it governs. Follow a code for scope and test conditions.</p>
      <h2>How to use this index</h2>
      <p>Use the table to identify the standard that governs the performance claim you are checking — cranking, capacity, cycle life, abuse tolerance or transport. Then open the standard page for scope, chemistry and the questions to ask a supplier. The index is organised by code, not by importance; a standard's presence here does not imply that every supplier claiming it has been tested against the current edition.</p>
      <h2>What the index does not do</h2>
      <p>It does not reproduce the standard text, certify a product, or replace a test report. Pass/fail parameters, tolerances and sample conditioning belong to the published standard; the manufacturer's report should state the edition and the clause tested.</p>

      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Standard</th><th>Region</th><th>Chemistry</th><th>Object</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((s) => (
            <tr>
              <td><a href={`/standards/${s.id}/`}>{s.code}</a></td>
              <td>{s.region}</td>
              <td>{s.chemistry}</td>
              <td>{s.object}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">Machine-readable versions: <a href="/standards.json">standards.json</a> · <a href="/standards.csv">standards.csv</a></p>
      <p class="muted">Reference information; the published standard text governs test parameters.</p>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>
